  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "os.system with f-string command (rm -rf {directory}/old/*) enables command injection if directory is untrusted; unsafe shell usage and destructive filesystem operation.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "subprocess.call(..., shell=True) with interpolated host/path enables command injection; static evidence: AST_DANGEROUS_SUBPROCESS_SHELL at line 8.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "os.popen(user_input) directly executes arbitrary shell input from the caller, allowing arbitrary command execution.",
      "category": "command-injection",
      "severity": "critical"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "fix_description": "Apply safe shell-free fixes: 1) cleanup_old_files: replace os.system(f\"rm -rf {directory}/old/*\") with pathlib to limit scope and subprocess.run([...]) using non-shell argv; e.g. resolve target dir and call shutil.rmtree or subprocess.run([\"rm\",\"-rf\",\"--\",str(target)]). 2) run_backup: replace subprocess.call(f\"scp -r {path} user@{host}:/backup\", shell=True) with subprocess.run([\"scp\",\"-r\",\"--\",path,f\"{user}@{host}:/backup\"]) to avoid shell interpolation and option injection. 3) execute_command: remove os.popen, use subprocess.run with an explicit argv list (e.g. shlex.split(user_input)) and no shell, or better restrict to a predefined allowlist of commands; avoid executing arbitrary shell syntax. All three should be refactored to parameterized, non-shell execution with input validation.",
